<h2>The prosecution&rsquo;s case</h2>
<p>Counterfeit notes were recovered from two co-accused on 22 September 2023. The applicant was arrested two days later in Ajmer, with counterfeit currency of Rs 1,00,000 allegedly recovered from him. The State&rsquo;s case went further than possession: it painted him as the central figure of a counterfeit-printing syndicate — the printing setup was recovered at his instance, a purchase invoice for it bore his name, and he was named in other FIRs in Rajasthan.</p>
<p>The defence pressed over three years of custody and a structural stall in the trial: an unresolved question whether the case must be tried by a Special Court under Section 22 of the National Investigation Agency Act, 2008, given the nature of counterfeit-currency offences.</p>

<h2>Three applications, one standard</h2>
<p>The first bail application was dismissed on merits on 24 July 2025; the second was withdrawn on 10 March 2026. For the third, the question was whether anything material had changed. The court found nothing: the material recovery witnesses were still unexamined, and the applicant&rsquo;s role remained as alleged. On parity, the court applied <em>Ramesh Bhavan Rathod</em> — parity turns on the role attributed to each accused, not on the bare fact that a co-accused is out — and on antecedents and the risk of reoffending it drew on <em>Prasanta Kumar Sarkar</em>. Trial delay was weighed, but held relevant rather than decisive in the face of the alleged central role.</p>

<h2>Case management instead of release</h2>
<p>What distinguishes the order is its remedial design. Rather than a bare dismissal, the court built a timetable:</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. The forum question, resolved fast.</strong> The trial court must decide within four weeks whether the matter belongs before a Special Court under Section 22 of the NIA Act — the issue that had been stalling progress.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Recovery witnesses on short dates.</strong> If the trial continues, the material recovery witnesses are to be examined expeditiously. As the court put it, &ldquo;the ends of justice would be met by directing the expeditious examination of the material recovery witnesses&rdquo;.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. A defined right to return.</strong> &ldquo;The petitioner shall be at liberty to renew his prayer for bail after the material recovery witnesses have been examined&rdquo; — or after six months, whichever is earlier.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why this structure matters</h2>
<p>Counterfeit currency cases sit at an uncomfortable intersection: offences grave enough to be scheduled under the NIA Act, trials that often hinge on a handful of recovery witnesses, and accused persons whose custody lengthens while forum questions linger. The order channels all three pressures productively. The prosecution keeps its custody of an alleged syndicate head, but on a clock; the defence loses the application but gains a concrete, near-term trigger for renewal; and the trial court receives directions that convert an open-ended delay into a four-week decision and a short-date witness schedule.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>For practitioners:</strong> where a successive bail application is weak on changed circumstances, consider asking in the alternative for trial-expedition directions with liberty to renew. Courts increasingly prefer structured timelines to repeated, unstructured bail litigation.</p>
</div>

<h2>The BNSS frame</h2>
<p>Regular bail before the High Court is now governed by Section 483 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023, corresponding to Section 439 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973. The substantive offences here remain under the IPC because the FIR dates to 2023; for conduct after 1 July 2024, counterfeiting of currency is dealt with under Section 178 and following of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023. The principles governing successive applications, parity and delay are unchanged by the transition.</p>
